\section*{The universal radius containing all but m electrons of neutral atoms}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Neutral-atom exterior-mass radii are claimed to have the Thomas--Fermi asymptotic $(81\pi^2/2)^{1/3}m^{-1/3}$ in the stated large-charge limits.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For each integer nuclear charge \(Z\), let \(\Psi_Z\) be any normalized neutral ground state of
\[
 H_Z=\sum_{i=1}^Z(-\Delta_i/2-Z/|x_i|)+\sum_{i<j}|x_i-x_j|^{-1}
\]
on the spin-two antisymmetric space. With spin-summed density \(\rho_{\Psi_Z}\), define \(R_m(\Psi_Z)=\inf\{r:\int_{|x|>r}\rho_{\Psi_Z}\le m\}\). The source claims
\[
 \lim_{m\to\infty}m^{1/3}\limsup_{Z\to\infty,\ Z>m}R_m(\Psi_Z)
 =\lim_{m\to\infty}m^{1/3}\liminf_{Z\to\infty,\ Z>m}R_m(\Psi_Z)
 =\left(\frac{81\pi^2}{2}\right)^{1/3}.
\]
The inner limit holds \(m\) fixed; the state remains neutral and has \(Z\), rather than \(Z-m\), electrons. Both $Z$ and $m$ tend through positive integers in the iterated limits.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Conditional screening data are retained on expanding annuli. Compactness and a uniqueness argument identify the neutral limiting profile, whose integrated electron tails then determine both the upper and lower radius limits uniformly over ground states.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Neutral-atom exterior-mass radii are claimed to have the Thomas--Fermi asymptotic $(81\pi^2/2)^{1/3}m^{-1/3}$ in the stated large-charge limits. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 263, the documented entries give the large-$m$ ionization-energy coefficient and the generalized neutral outer-radius asymptotics. Fixed-$m$ limits and the general uniform excess-charge manuscript are not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{CoulombIonization.lean}, \texttt{CoulombRadii.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CoulombAtom.generalized\_ionization}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

